{"title":"2024 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 17","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Complementary_Counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Complementary Counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_DDM\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by DDM</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_understand_the_problem!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_(super_clear!)_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2  (super clear!) by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_4_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 4 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_NiuniuMaths_(Easy_to_understand!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_CosineMethod_[🔥Fast_and_Easy🔥]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by CosineMethod [🔥Fast and Easy🔥]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Dr._David\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Dr. David</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A chess king is said to attack all the squares one step away from it, horizontally, vertically, or diagonally. For instance, a king on the center square of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> x <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> grid attacks all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> other squares, as shown below. Suppose a white king and a black king are placed on different squares of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> x <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> grid so that they do not attack each other (in other words, not right next to each other). In how many ways can this be done?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/b/acb4a58e7357f1e83d149b52bcceabbdf33cac4d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(29pt); import math; add(grid(3,3));  pair [] a = {(0.5,0.5), (0.5, 1.5), (0.5, 2.5), (1.5, 2.5), (2.5,2.5), (2.5,1.5), (2.5,0.5), (1.5,0.5)};  for (int i=0; i&lt;a.length; ++i) {     pair x = (1.5,1.5) + 0.4*dir(225-45*i);     draw(x -- a[i], arrow=EndArrow()); }  label(&quot;$K$&quot;, (1.5,1.5)); [/asy]\" width=\"148\" height=\"148\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73a90a7194bf033ab1f928bc366ed261dc80303.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 20 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 24 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 27 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 28 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"397\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you place a king in any of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> corners, the other king will have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> spots to go and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> corners, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/d/09d5dcbff39099361cc0bf85d5fa603db2067483.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 4=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" />.\nIf you place a king in any of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges, the other king will have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> spots to go and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/8/af85e67238f3076d072a408df3202a98b7c8462d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 4=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" />. \nThat gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/9/0b9d49628dd6d288b70421b6e2591b8bdc47411b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20+12=32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" /> spots for the other king to go into in total.\nSo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5c81f1f2c1232b163ae0066e5e924c2c63e6769.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} 32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"59\" height=\"30\" /> is the answer.\n~andliu766, captaindiamond868 (minor edits)\n~AliceDubbleYou\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We see that the center is not a viable spot for either of the kings to be in, as it would attack all nearby squares.\n</p><p>This gives three combinations: \n</p><p>Corner-corner: There are 4 corners, and none of them are touching orthogonally or diagonally, so it's <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/9/5c9b68a04fadb4cbe90954c537de424c1519ef51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{4}{2}=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"70\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Corner-edge: For each corner, there are two edges that don't border it, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/4/6f40e5d9698ac8f7f58e674d2c9aaf86b19f104c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot2=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"64\" height=\"13\" /> \n</p><p>Edge-edge: The only possible combinations of this that work are top-bottom and left-right edges, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for this type\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/4/764b4e58fdf78e924cf51d668d99c5119db8d735.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6+8+2=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" /> \n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Complementary_Counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Complementary_Counting)\">Solution 3 (Complementary Counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Complementary Counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We see that the center is off-limits for the kings. Additionally, the kings cannot be next to each other. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> ways for the kings to be placed adjacently: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5b4928c8fe50ce3c2428da3bee3505e891b788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> corner-edge permutations and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edge-edge permutations. In total, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b3188f6be4b5edec47a74bccc067940c09f3723d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8P2=56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"77\" height=\"14\" /> ways to arrange the kings conditions aside. Therefore, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/6/d16e1bddafbcbe2fa98cecff79365d4754da4a28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$56-24=$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"80\" height=\"15\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5c81f1f2c1232b163ae0066e5e924c2c63e6769.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} 32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"59\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~abirgh\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ\">https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_DDM\">Video Solution by DDM</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by DDM\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg\">https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg</a>\n</p><p>~piggydude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_.28Goes_through_full_thought_process.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/eQ6UUmZuhhY\">https://youtu.be/eQ6UUmZuhhY</a>\n</p><p>~mr_mathman\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_.28First_understand_the_problem.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_understand_the_problem!!!)\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=Q2e8OfkuzKZXmoau&amp;t=4624\">https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=Q2e8OfkuzKZXmoau&amp;t=4624</a>\n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28A_Clever_Explanation_You.E2.80.